{"step_index":330,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T20:29:02Z","content":"Done! Here's what was implemented:\n\n### Virtual Joystick (Drone Controller)\n\n**HTML** (`index.html`):\n- Imported the **nipplejs** library via CDN.\n- Added a `joystick-zone` div positioned at the **bottom-left** of the 3D viewer (`absolute bottom-10 left-10 w-32 h-32 z-50`).\n\n**JavaScript** (`app.js`):\n- Initialized nipplejs in `static` mode with a **cyan (#06b6d4)** color to match the SpaceX HUD aesthetic. The joystick renders as a 100px thumbstick inside the zone.\n- On `move` events, the joystick's normalized X/Y vector is captured into `joystickInput`. Dragging the stick also resets `lastInteractionTime`, which prevents the auto-rotation from fighting you.\n- On `end` (release), the input resets to zero and the stick snaps back to center.\n- Inside the unified `viewer.clock.onTick` listener, the joystick input is checked **first**. If active and no entity is tracked, it smoothly rotates the camera using `rotateRight(x * 0.02)` and `rotateUp(y * 0.02)`. The idle auto-rotation only kicks in after 4 seconds of **no** interaction (joystick, mouse, or wheel).\n\n**Please refresh your browser at http://localhost:3000**, open the 3D Orbit Viewer tab, and drag the cyan thumbstick in the bottom-left corner to smoothly fly the camera around the Earth like a drone!"}
{"step_index":331,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-27T08:22:16Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nThe satellite is detached from its orbit line because separate coordinate frames are being mixed: the polyline was rendered statically in an inertial frame (ECI) while the satellite position is evaluating dynamically in Earth-fixed coordinates (ECEF). The TCA zoom black screen happens because the two objects are only ~1.2 km apart, so flyTo attempts to frame a tiny bounding box and collapses the camera distance to near zero.\n\nCopy and paste this prompt into Antigravity to fix both root causes:\n\n\"We have two visual bugs in the 3D Orbit Viewer:\n\nSatellites are visually drifting off their orbit lines (e.g., STARLINK-1007 is floating off-track).\n\nThe 'Jump to TCA' feature zooms in so aggressively that the camera clips through the entities and renders a black screen.\n\nPlease update app.js with the following targeted fixes:\n\n1. Lock Orbit Lines to Satellite Position (Cesium.PathGraphics)\nStop drawing separate, static PolylineGraphics for orbits. When the globe rotates, manual polyline arrays drift away from the propagated satellite coordinates.\n\nAttach a dynamic path directly to each satellite entity so it natively reads from entity.position:\n\nJavaScript\nentity.path = new Cesium.PathGraphics({\n    leadTime: 3600,   // Draw 60 min ahead\n    trailTime: 3600,  // Draw 60 min behind\n    width: 1.5,\n    resolution: 120,\n    material: new Cesium.PolylineGlowMaterialProperty({\n        glowPower: 0.15,\n        color: isDebris ? Cesium.Color.RED.withAlpha(0.7) : Cesium.Color.CYAN.withAlpha(0.7)\n    })\n});\nThis guarantees the satellite is mathematically anchored to its orbital track at all times.\n\n2. Fix the TCA Zoom Blackout (Safe Bounding Sphere Framing)\nIn the jumpToTCA() handler:\n\nSet viewer.trackedEntity = undefined; (trackedEntity locks collapse camera distance to zero on tight conjunctions).\n\nSample the positions of both objects at the exact TCA timestamp:\n\nJavaScript\nconst posA = objA.position.getValue(tcaTime);\nconst posB = objB.position.getValue(tcaTime);\nCompute the midpoint between them:\n\nJavaScript\nconst midpoint = Cesium.Cartesian3.midpoint(posA, posB, new Cesium.Cartesian3());\nFly the camera using a safe, clamped BoundingSphere that enforces a minimum viewing distance of 450 km:\n\nJavaScript\nconst encounterSphere = new Cesium.BoundingSphere(midpoint, 150000); // 150 km bounding radius\nviewer.camera.flyToBoundingSphere(encounterSphere, {\n    duration: 1.8,\n    offset: new Cesium.HeadingPitchRange(\n        viewer.camera.heading,\n        Cesium.Math.toRadians(-35), // 35-degree downward angle showing Earth\n        500000                      // Fixed safe distance: 500 km away\n    )\n});\nAction: Apply these fixes to app.js. Confirm that STARLINK-1007 rides directly on its orbital line, and clicking 'JUMP TO TCA' glides the camera to a well-framed 500 km vantage point showing both satellites and the Earth without clipping.\"\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-27T13:52:16+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
